-BaM7OCEm3G0.youtube,Cross products as dual linear functionals: a duality-driven view in 3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra (Chapter 11),"August 31, 2016",,"This video presents a rigorous, principle-based route to understanding the 3D cross product by viewing it through the lens of linear transformations and duality. It begins with the familiar determinant-based construction of the cross product, where the second and third columns are the vectors v and w and the first column holds the symbolic basis vectors i-hat, j-hat, k-hat. By treating the resulting determinant as the coordinates of a resulting vector, it connects the familiar geometric facts—the cross product’s magnitude equals the parallelogram area spanned by v and w, and its direction is perpendicular to both with orientation given by the right-hand rule—to a deeper, dual-interpretive framework. The video then introduces duality for transformations from three dimensions to the reals, showing that a linear map to the number line can be represented as a dot product with a special vector p. By applying this idea to the determinant-based functional det[(x, y, z), v, w], the speaker derives p as the dual vector, tying the computational determinant trick to a geometric vector perpendicular to v and w with length equal to the parallelogram area. This dual viewpoint explains why the same computation produces the cross product and clarifies the projection-interpretation of the dot product with p, ultimately linking the algebraic and geometric pictures and foreshadowing the change of basis theme to come in the next video.","3D cross product; determinant method for cross product; i-hat, j-hat, k-hat in determinant first column; cross product magnitude equals parallelogram area; cross product direction and right-hand rule; duality in linear transformations; transformation from R^3 to R as a dot product with a dual vector; 1x3 matrix representation of a linear map to the real line; determinant as a linear function of (x, y, z); dual vector p for cross product; geometric interpretation of the dual vector; projection interpretation: volume via area × perpendicular component; equivalence of computational and geometric viewpoints; change of basis groundwork (foreshadowed)","Determinant-based cross product construction with i-hat, j-hat, k-hat placeholders @ 00:00:16; Right-hand rule for cross product orientation @ 00:01:28; Duality: map from R^3 to R represented as dot product with a dual vector @ 00:02:04; Identify dual vector p from determinant coefficients @ 00:06:46; Geometric interpretation: p perpendicular to v and w with length equal to parallelogram area @ 00:10:24; Equivalence of determinant computation and dot-product projection with p @ 00:11:35",5
-Ip3X9LOh2dk.youtube,Determinant: Visualizing Area and Volume Scaling with Orientation (2D and 3D),"August 10, 2016",,"This video builds an intuition for the determinant as the single scaling factor that a linear transformation applies to area in the plane (and to volume in space). Through concrete 2x2 and 3x3 examples, it shows how basis vectors are transformed, how an area changes for a unit square and a unit cube, and how orientation can flip when the determinant is negative. It also introduces the idea that the determinant is zero exactly when space collapses to a lower dimension, and it provides the 2x2 formula ad − bc with geometric intuition, before hinting at the multiplicative property det(AB) = det(A)det(B).","Determinant concept as area/volume scaling; Area scaling under linear transformations; Unit square to rectangle under a matrix; Shear transformation and parallelogram area; Orientation and sign of determinants (positive/negative); Right-hand rule for 3D orientation; Determinant zero and dimensional collapse; 2x2 determinant formula ad − bc; Intuition behind determinant terms a, b, c, d; 3x3 determinant as volume scaling (unit cube to parallelepiped); Determinant of product property det(AB) = det(A)det(B); Linear independence and determinant (columns); Grid-argument for uniform area changes; Parallelepiped terminology in 3D","2x2 transformation: scaling of basis vectors resulting in a 3-by-2 area change (columns (3,0) and (0,2)) @ 00:00:51.320; Shear transformation: unit square becoming a parallelogram under the matrix with columns (1,0) and (1,1) @ 00:01:22.000; Determinant as global area-scaling factor demonstrated via grid of squares @ 00:02:28.000; Negative determinant indicating orientation flip in 2D @ 00:03:54.000; 3D extension: unit cube mapped to a parallelepiped and volume scaled by determinant @ 00:06:04.000; 2x2 determinant formula derivation intuition (ad − bc) @ 00:07:31.000; Product property of determinants: det(AB) = det(A)det(B) @ 00:09:18.000",7
-LyGKycYT2v0.youtube,"Dot products and duality: projection, matrices, and the transformation view","August 24, 2016",,"In this 3Blue1Brown segment from Essence of Linear Algebra, the dot product is revisited not as a stand-alone computation but as a gateway to projections, linear transformations, and duality. The talk starts from the standard coordinate-wise definition and geometric projection interpretation, covers how dot products reflect whether vectors point in the same direction, are perpendicular, or oppose each other, and discusses the symmetry of the operation. It then builds a bridge to the matrix view by treating a 2D-to-1D transformation as a 1-by-2 matrix, illustrated with the Ihat, Jhat basis and a concrete example. A key move is embedding a number line diagonally in 2D space to show how a unit-vector projection corresponds to a 1-by-2 matrix acting on vectors, which leads to the core idea of duality: certain 1D-linear transformations correspond to specific vectors in the plane, and vice versa. The video culminates in a conceptual synthesis—dot products as a way to translate vectors into the world of transformations—before alluding to the cross product in the next video.",dot product; coordinate-wise product; projection of W onto V; positive / negative / zero dot product; orthogonality; order symmetry of dot product; linear transformation from R^2 to R; 1x2 matrix representation; i_hat and j_hat basis vectors; matrix-vector multiplication; dot product as a projection onto a unit vector; span of a unit vector; projection length; non-unit vectors and scaling in dot products; duality in linear algebra; vectors as transformations; embedding the number line in 2D space; dual of a vector vs dual of a transformation; visual interpretation of duality,Projection interpretation of the dot product onto a unit vector @ 00:10:25; 1x2 matrix representation of a linear transformation to a scalar @ 00:05:52; Duality: translating vectors into linear transformations @ 00:12:24; Embedding a number line diagonally in 2D space to define a linear transformation @ 00:07:35; Symmetry argument for dot product order independence @ 00:02:45; Non-unit vector projection and scaling interpretation @ 00:10:50,4
-P2LTAUO1TdA.youtube,Change of Basis in Linear Algebra: Translating Vectors Between Coordinate Systems (Jennifer’s Basis),"September 11, 2016",,"3Blue1Brown’s chapter on Change of Basis explains how vectors and coordinates depend on the chosen basis. The video introduces standard basis vectors i-hat and j-hat, then presents Jennifer’s alternate basis b1 = (2,1) and b2 = (-1,1). It shows how a vector described in our coordinates (3,2) can be expressed in Jennifer’s coordinates as (5/3, 1/3) by forming the change-of-basis matrix from Jennifer’s basis and applying it to the vector. The inverse matrix translates in the opposite direction, enabling us to convert (3,2) to Jennifer’s language with coordinates (5/3, 1/3). The talk then connects these ideas to matrix-vector multiplication, viewing a matrix whose columns are Jennifer’s basis vectors as a transformation that maps our standard basis to Jennifer’s. It demonstrates how to rotate space (90-degree CCW) and how to compute the corresponding transformation in Jennifer’s language via A^{-1} M A, illustrating the idea of “empathy” between coordinate systems. The section concludes by tying these constructions to the general utility of changing basis and hints at eigenvectors and eigenvalues in a subsequent video. ","basis vectors; i-hat and j-hat; coordinate systems; Jennifer’s basis b1=(2,1) and b2=(-1,1); change-of-basis matrix (columns as basis vectors); inverse change-of-basis matrix; translating coordinates between bases (e.g., (3,2) ↔ (5/3,1/3)); matrix-vector multiplication as basis transformation; rotation by 90 degrees and its matrix in different bases; A^{-1} M A triple matrix form; empathy view of coordinate transformations; transforming between languages of coordinates; geometric vs. numeric descriptions of transformations; computational steps for higher dimensions (inverse used); link to eigenvectors/eigenvalues in next video; visualizing grids vs. intrinsic space","Matrix-vector multiplication interpreted as a change of basis using columns that are the new basis vectors (Jennifer’s basis) @ 00:05:39.680; Using the inverse change-of-basis matrix to translate from our coordinates to Jennifer’s coordinates @ 00:07:18.120; Computing Jennifer’s coordinates for a given vector by applying the inverse change-of-basis matrix (example: (3,2) → (5/3, 1/3)) @ 00:08:16.685; Constructing the rotation matrix in Jennifer’s language via A^{-1} M A @ 00:10:39.680; Interpreting A^{-1} M A as the same transformation seen through a different coordinate language (empathy) @ 00:11:04.300",3
-PFDu9oVAE-g.youtube,Eigenvectors and eigenvalues: invariant directions and diagonalization,"September 15, 2016",,"3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra Chapter 14 visually introduces eigenvectors and eigenvalues as the invariant directions of a linear transformation. The video works through a concrete 2D example where the transformation stretches along specific lines (the x-axis and a diagonal line), then formalizes the eigenvector equation Av = λv, leading to the standard determinant-based eigenvalue computation det(A − λI) = 0. It shows how to extract eigenvectors by solving (A − λI)v = 0, gives concrete eigenvectors for the example (e.g., along (1,0) and (−1,1)), and discusses special cases such as rotations (which may have no real eigenvectors) and shear (limited eigenvectors). The concept of an eigenbasis is introduced, explaining how if a matrix has enough eigenvectors, changing to that basis diagonalizes the transformation, making computations such as high powers of A straightforward. The video also connects these ideas to change of basis and emphasizes when diagonalization is beneficial (and when it isn’t).","Eigenvectors; Eigenvalues; linear transformations; span; basis vectors; Av = λv; (A − λI)v = 0; det(A − λI) = 0; characteristic equation; diagonalization; eigenbasis; change of basis; P^{-1}AP = D; Diagonal matrices; powers of a matrix; rotation vs. eigenvectors; shear and eigenvectors; axis of rotation; invariant directions; 2D example matrix [[3,1],[0,2]]; eigenvectors directions (1,0) and (−1,1); invariant lines and spans",Deriving eigenvalues via det(A − λI) = 0 (characteristic equation) @ 00:06:01; Finding eigenvectors from (A − λI)v = 0 @ 00:07:06; Diagonalization into eigenbasis: P^{-1}AP = D @ 00:15:03; Using eigenbasis to compute powers (A^n) easily @ 00:16:07,8
-TgKwz5Ikpc8.youtube,Polynomials as Vectors: Representing the Derivative as a Linear Operator,"September 24, 2016",,"In this episode from the Essence of Linear Algebra, the author broadens the notion of a vector beyond arrows or lists of numbers to include function spaces, and then uses polynomials as a concrete, infinite-dimensional example. Functions can be added and scaled just like vectors, and linear transformations (operators) preserve these two operations. A basis for polynomials is chosen using powers of x, rendering polynomials as coordinate vectors with infinite length (only finitely many nonzero terms). The derivative operator is then shown to be linear, and with respect to the polynomial basis it is represented by an infinite, mostly-zero matrix that shifts coefficients down the diagonal. A practical demonstration on a specific polynomial shows how the derivative corresponds to a matrix-vector product in this basis. The video culminates by framing linear algebra as a general theory that applies to many vector-like objects (arrows, numbers, functions, etc.) via a shared axiomatic structure of vector spaces and linear transformations.","vectors; vectors as arrows; lists of numbers; vector spaces; basis; coordinates; polynomials; infinite-dimensional spaces; basis {1, x, x^2, ...}; functions as vectors; function addition; scalar multiplication; linear transformations; operators; additivity; scaling; derivative as a linear operator; matrix representation of a linear operator; derivative matrix in polynomial basis; basis-dependent matrices; matrix-vector multiplication; eigenfunctions / eigenvectors; axioms of vector spaces; abstraction in linear algebra","Treating functions as vectors with addition and scalar multiplication @ 00:02:19; Defining linear transformations via additivity and scaling @ 00:03:46; Constructing a derivative operator matrix in a polynomial basis @ 00:09:06; Using an infinite, basis-driven coordinate representation for polynomials @ 00: