-XkY2DOUCWMU.youtube,"Matrix multiplication as composition: interpreting linear transformations via basis vectors (Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 4)","August 8, 2016",,"3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 4, presents matrix multiplication as the composition of linear transformations. The video recalls that a linear transformation is determined by the images of the basis vectors i-hat and j-hat, so the matrix columns encode these transformed bases. It then shows how composing two transformations (e.g., rotation followed by a shear) yields a single matrix—the product—that captures the overall effect. The derivation is illustrated both geometrically and numerically: you can obtain the composition matrix by tracking where each basis vector ends up through the sequence, or by applying the left matrix to each column of the right matrix. The right-to-left reading of products follows from function notation. The chapter also covers the order-sensitivity of composition, demonstrates a concrete 2×2 example, generalizes to symbolic matrices, discusses associativity from a transformation viewpoint, and ends by encouraging conceptual understanding over memorization while foreshadowing higher-dimensional extensions in the next video.",linear transformations; matrix representation; basis vectors i-hat and j-hat; columns of a matrix as transformed basis vectors; matrix-vector multiplication; composition of linear transformations; product of matrices; order of multiplication (right-to-left); geometric interpretation of matrix products; rotation followed by shear; computing composition matrices from M1 and M2; column-by-column computation; symbolic matrix entries; associativity of matrix multiplication; transformation-based intuition vs rote computation; extending to higher dimensions; visual intuition in linear algebra; function notation and composition,Matrix representation from transformed basis vectors (columns = images of i-hat and j-hat) @ 00:00:46; Composition 