-e50Bj7jn9IQ.youtube,A quick trick for computing eigenvalues (2×2) from trace and determinant,"May 7, 2021",,"This 3Blue1Brown micro-lesson presents a fast method for finding eigenvalues of 2×2 matrices by using two already-available invariants: the trace (sum of eigenvalues) and the determinant (product of eigenvalues). The video shows that if you let the eigenvalues be λ1 and λ2, their mean m equals half the trace and their product p equals the determinant; then λ1 and λ2 are m ± sqrt(m^2 − p). After deriving and illustrating the method, the host works through concrete examples, including a quick matrix (3,1;4,1) and a second example (2,7;1,8), and then connects the idea to Pauli spin matrices in quantum mechanics. The video concludes with a comparison to the traditional characteristic-polynomial approach and discusses when this mean-and-product trick provides a speed advantage, especially for linear combinations of Pauli matrices. The segment also references a jingle by A Capella Science to mnemonic-ify the formula and ties the result back to broader quadratic-root ideas.",Eigenvalues; Eigenvectors; 2x2 matrices; Trace of a matrix; Determinant of a matrix; Mean of eigenvalues; Product of eigenvalues; λ = m ± sqrt(m^2 − p); m = trace(A)/2; p = det(A); Characteristic polynomial; Quadratic formula; Pauli spin matrices; Quantum mechanics; Diagonal matrices; Linear combinations of Pauli matrices; Normalization (a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 1); A Capella Science mnemonic; Relation between trace/determinant and eigenvalues,"Eigenvalues from mean and product: λ1,2 = m ± sqrt(m^2 − p) with m = trace(A)/2 and p = det(A) @ 00:04:41.220; Direct extraction of mean and product from a matrix: mean(λ) = mean(diagonal entries) and product(λ) = det(A) @ 00:02:14.673; Eigenvalues of Pauli spin matrices are ±1 with mean 0 and determinant −1 @ 00:07:29.700",4
-eu6i7WJeinw.youtube,"Cross products explained: 2D area intuition, 3D vector result, and a determinant-based construction","August 31, 2016",,"This two-part module opener surveys the standard 2D cross product intuition as the signed area of the parallelogram spanned by two vectors, emphasizing how orientation (order of V and W) determines the sign and how a determinant computes that area via the matrix with V and W as columns. The video then shifts to the true 3D cross product, describing it as a vector whose length equals the parallelogram area and whose direction is perpendicular to the plane, determined by the right-hand rule. A mnemonic determinant procedure in 3D is introduced: form a matrix with I, J, K in the first column and the coordinates of V and W in the other columns, then take its determinant to obtain the cross product vector. The host notes deeper geometric ideas (duality) in a follow-up video and clarifies the distinction between the 2D signed-area intuition and the full 3D cross product.","Cross product (2D) as signed area; Parallelogram spanned by two vectors; Orientation and sign of cross product; Order sensitivity: V × W vs W × V; Basis vectors I_hat and J_hat; 2x2 determinant with V and W as columns; Linear transformation sending I_hat, J_hat to V, W; Determinant as area scaling under transformation; Unit square transforming to a parallelogram; Example: V = (-3, 1), W = (2, 1) with determinant -5; Right-hand rule for cross product direction; Cross product as a 3D vector with length equal to parallelogram area; Direction of cross product perpendicular to the plane; 3D determinant trick using I_hat, J_hat, K_hat in the first column; Columns in the determinant reflect basis vectors; Connection to duality (brief mention for deeper study)","2D cross product treated as signed area of a parallelogram @ 00:01:04; Determinant with V and W as columns to compute 2D cross product @ 00:02:40; Determinant as area scaling via transformation of the unit square