-fNk_zzaMoSs.youtube,"Vectors: representations, coordinates, and the basics of addition and scaling","August 5, 2016",,"This video introduces the vector as a foundational concept in linear algebra, exploring three related perspectives (physics, computer science, and mathematics) and emphasizing the origin-centered geometric view. It explains how vectors can be seen as arrows in space or as ordered lists of numbers, and it shows how a vector in 2D is represented by coordinates (x, y) anchored at the origin, with a brief extension to 3D. The core operations—vector addition (via the tip-to-tail method) and scalar multiplication (scaling)—are defined and illustrated, including how coordinates transform under these operations. The video also discusses the intuition behind these operations, why they are defined the way they are, and how the two representations (space-and-geometry versus numerical lists) interrelate. It ends by foreshadowing more advanced topics like spans, bases, and linear dependence in subsequent videos.","vector; physics perspective; computer science perspective; mathematician perspective; arrows in space; origin/root of vectors; x-axis; y-axis; z-axis; coordinate system; coordinates; ordered lists; vector as [x, y]; grid lines; 2D space; 3D space; origin anchoring; tail-to-tip vector addition; geometric interpretation of addition; component-wise addition; vectors as lists of numbers; scaling / scalar multiplication; stretching / shrinking; negative scaling; scalar vs number; interchange between representations; data visualization; graphics and physics applications; upcoming topics: span, bases, linear dependence",Tip-to-tail vector addition (geometric construction) @ 00:04:56; Componentwise vector addition from coordinates @ 00:06:40; Scalar multiplication: scaling and its effects on length and direction @ 00:07:01; Interpreting scalar multiplication as stretching/shrinking @ 00:07:13; Connecting vector representations: arrows vs lists @ 00:08:20,7
-jBsC34PxzoM.youtube,"Geometric derivation of Cramer's rule (2x2 and beyond) | Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 12","March 16, 2019",,"3Blue1Brown presents a geometric take on solving linear systems with two unknowns using Cramer’s rule. Framing the problem as a matrix transforming an input vector to a known output, the lecture explores how determinants encode area (and, in 3D, volume) scaling under a linear transformation. The video walks through the idea that coordinates can be recovered from areas associated with parallelograms (and parallelepipeds in higher dimensions), and shows how replacing columns in the coefficient matrix yields the determinants used to compute each coordinate. It emphasizes that Cramer’s rule is not the fastest computational method (Gaussian elimination is typically quicker) but serves as a deep, visual intuition linking determinants, dot products, and linear systems, with a concrete 2×2 numeric example and a nod toward higher dimensions.)","Cramer’s rule; Determinants; Matrix as linear transformation; basis vectors i-hat and j-hat; Parallelogram area representing coordinates (2D); Parallelepiped volume representing coordinates (3D); Determinant as uniform area/volume scaling; Determinants with replaced columns (A_x, A_y); Nonzero determinant implying invertibility; Higher-dimensional generalization (3x3, etc); Orthogonality and dot product preservation in special cases; Gaussian elimination versus Cramer’s rule (conceptual vs computational); Right-hand rule and determinant sign; Geometric intuition for linear systems; Consolidation of linear algebra concepts (determinant, dot product, linear systems)",Cramer's rule derivation via determinants with replaced columns (A_x and A_y) @ 00:10:13; Determinant as a uniform area/volume scaling under a linear transformation @ 00:08:09; Parallelogram/Parallelepiped coordinates interpretation of vector components @ 00:05:50; Dot product preservation in orthonormal transforms as a special case @ 00:0