-k7RM-ot2NWY.youtube,"Linear combinations, span, and basis vectors: core ideas in Chapter 2 (Essence of Linear Algebra, 3Blue1Brown)","August 6, 2016",,"This video establishes how coordinates in a vector space can be understood as scalars multiplying basis vectors, introduces the notions of span, linear combinations, and linear (in)dependence, and explains how the choice of basis alters the coordinate description. Using the familiar i-hat and j-hat for 2D, it shows when a set of vectors spans the entire space, reduces to a line, or collapses to a single point. The discussion then extends to three dimensions, illustrating how adding a non-coplanar vector expands the span from a plane to all of space, and it defines what a basis is (a linearly independent spanning set). The video concludes by foreshadowing matrices in transforming space.",i-hat (unit x-basis vector); j-hat (unit y-basis vector); basis of a coordinate system; scalars as coordinates; linear combination; span; plane vs line vs origin; two vectors in 2D; non-colinear vectors in 3D; span in 3D; three-vector span; linear dependence; linear independence; redundant vectors; basis definition; coordinates depend on basis; visual interpretation: vectors as arrows vs points; matrices in transforming space (preview),"Expressing a vector as a linear combination of basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat) @ 00:00:32; Visualizing span via one-parameter variation producing a line @ 00:03:18; Conceptualizing span as the set of all linear combinations @ 00:04:02; Differentiating between plane-span, line-span, and origin when dealing with 2D vectors @ 00:04:29; Extending span intuition from 2D to 3D via a spanning sheet @ 00:06:04; Determining linear dependence vs independence as a criterion for a basis @ 00:08:16; Basis as a linearly independent spanning set @ 00:09:06",12
-kYB8IZa5AuE.youtube,2D Linear Transformations and their Matrix Representation (i-hat and j-hat),"August 7, 2016",,"This video introduces linear transformations in two dimensions and shows how every such transformation can be encoded by a 2x2 matrix. It emphasizes visual intuition by treating input vectors as points on a grid and watching how the entire space is mapped while preserving straight lines and fixing the origin. The key idea is that the transformation is completely determined by where the two basis vectors i-hat and j-hat land; those two image vectors form the columns of the describing matrix, and all other vectors are obtained by linear combinations of these images. Concrete examples include a 90-degree rotation and a shear, with a clear formula for how a general vector (x, y) is mapped to x times the image of i-hat plus y times the image of j-hat. The video culminates in taking away the perspective that matrices are transformations of space, a viewpoint that clarifies later topics like matrix multiplication, determinants, and eigenvalues.","linear transformation; basis vectors i-hat and j-hat; 2x2 matrices; matrix-vector multiplication; images of basis vectors as matrix columns; general mapping formula (ax+by, cx+dy); rotation by 90 degrees; shear transformation; grid visualization of transformations; origin fixed; parallelism preservation; linear independence and space squishing; interpretation of matrices as space transformations","Mapping of basis vectors i-hat and j-hat under a linear transformation (basis-image method) @ 00:04:49.727; Matrix-column representation: images of i-hat and j-hat form the two columns (ax+by, cx+dy) @ 00:07:33.980; 90-degree rotation matrix visualization (i-hat -> (0,1); j-hat -> (-1,0)) @ 00:08:13.980; Shear transformation construction (i-hat fixed; j-hat moves to (1,1)) @ 00:08:42.481; General transformation action (ax+by, cx+dy) derived from basis images @ 00:07:33.980; Deduction from images of basis to full transformation; concepts of linear independence and space mapping @ 00:09:21.680; Visualization principle: gridlines remain parallel and evenly spaced to visualize linearity @ 00:02:39.120",4
-rHLEWRxRGiM.youtube,"Three-Dimensional Linear Transformations: Basis Vectors, 3x3 Matrices, and Transformation Composition","August 9, 2016",,"This short footnote from 3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra expands the prior two-dimensional focus into three dimensions. It explains that a 3D linear transformation is fully described by the images of the three standard basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat, k-hat), whose coordinates form the columns of a 3×3 transformation matrix. The video shows how to visualize a 3D transform by tracking how basis vectors move, how to compute the image of an arbitrary vector via matrix-column scaling and summation, and how multiplying two 3×3 matrices corresponds to composing two transformations. It also highlights the practical significance of 3D transforms in fields like computer graphics and robotics, and it briefly points forward to the determinant as the next topic.","3D linear transformations; 3x3 transformation matrices; basis vectors: i-hat, j-hat, k-hat; images of basis vectors under a 3D transform; construction of a 3x3 matrix from basis coordinates; rotation in 3D (example: 90-degree rotation around the y-axis); applying a 3x3 matrix to a 3D vector; pre- and post-transformation scaling and addition; vector decomposition with respect to a basis; matrix multiplication in 3D; transformation composition (right-to-left order); applications in computer graphics; applications in robotics; determinant as a future topic; visualization of 3D grids under transformation","Describe 3D linear transformations via the images of the standard basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat, k-hat) and form the 3×3 matrix from their coordinates @ 01:56.920; Construct the 3×3 transformation matrix by recording the coordinates of i-hat, j-hat, and k-hat as its columns @ 02:11.260; Rotate 3D space by a 90-degree transformation around the y-axis as a concrete example @ 02:20.545; Compute the image of a 3D vector by multiplying its coordinates with the matrix columns and summing @ 02:56.940; Interpret matrix multiplication as the composition of two transformations (apply right-hand matrix first, then left-hand) @ 03:32.020; Recognize the practical relevance of 3D matrix multiplication for graphics and robotics @ 03:44.060",5
-uQhTuRlWMxw.youtube,"Inverse matrices, column space, and null space (Chapter 7) – Essence of Linear Algebra","August 15, 2016",,"This video builds intuition for inverse matrices, the column space, rank, and the null space through the geometric lens of linear transformations. It frames linear systems as Ax = V, where A is a matrix of coefficients, X the unknown vector, and V the right-hand side. The discussion covers when A is invertible (nonzero determinant) and how the inverse A^{-1} undoes A to solve Ax = V, emphasizing that in the nonzero-determinant case there is a unique solution obtained by applying the inverse to V. The video also introduces the concepts of the column space (the set of all possible outputs of the transformation), rank (the dimension of that output space), and the null space (the set of vectors that map to zero), and it highlights how these ideas explain existence and form of solutions in a geometric way. Throughout, the focus is on intuition rather than computational methods, noting that practical calculation is typically left to software and that Gaussian elimination/row-echelon methods lie outside the scope of this particular presentation. The content also touches on the use of linear algebra in applications like computer graphics and robotics, and it signals that a future short footnote will cover non-square matrices.","Ax = V as a linear transformation; A as a linear transformation; X as the variable vector; V as the right-hand side vector; Geometric interpretation of solving Ax = V; Determinant and invertibility; Nonzero determinant implies a unique solution; Inverse matrix A^{-1}; A^{-1}A = I and the identity transformation; Solving Ax = V by applying A^{-1} to V; Two- and three-variable systems (2x2, 3x3); Square matrices vs non-square matrices (scope clarification); Column space and rank; Rank as the dimension of the column space; Full rank and nonzero determinant; Null space (kernel) and solutions to Ax = 0; Zero determinant and noninvertible cases; When solutions exist without an inverse; Applications: computer graphics and robotics; Reference to external computation tools (Gaussian elimination/row echelon form); Future topics: non-square matrices and dot products under linear transformations",Viewing Ax = V as a geometric linear transformation problem @ 00:02:04; Using the matrix inverse to solve linear systems (A^{-1}V) @ 00:04:25; Identity transformation as the result of A^{-1}A @ 00:05:08; Determinant non-zero implies unique solution @ 00:03:54; Column space and rank intuition @ 00:08:02; Null space (kernel) as vectors mapping to zero @ 00:10:32,3
-v8VSDg_WQlA.youtube,"Non-square matrices as dimension-changing linear transformations (3Blue1Brown, Essence of Linear Algebra)","August 16, 2016",,"This video explains the geometric meaning of non-square matrices as linear transformations between spaces of different dimensions. It shows that a matrix encodes a map by recording where the basis vectors land, with the matrix shape indicating input and output dimensions (e.g., a 3×2 matrix maps 2D input to 3D output; a 2×3 matrix maps 3D input to 2D output). The discussion introduces the column space as the image of the transformation (a plane through the origin in the output space) and covers the notion of full rank in the context of these maps. It also demonstrates a 2D-to-1D case encoded by a 1×2 matrix and hints at the connection to the dot product, inviting further exploration of transformations between differing dimensions.",non-square matrices; linear transformations between different dimensions; input space vs output space; basis vectors; images of basis vectors; columns of a matrix; 3×2 matrix (2D → 3D mapping); 2×3 matrix (3D → 2D mapping); column space; plane through the origin; rank and full rank; transformation from 2D to 1D; dot product relation (hinted for next video); grid-line preservation and origin mapping,"Geometric interpretation of non-square matrices as dimension-changing linear transformations (example: 2D → 3D from 3Blue1Brown) @ 00:00:11; Encoding a transformation by images of basis vectors; columns specify landing coordinates (I_hat → (2, -1, -2); J_hat → (0, 1, 1)) @ 00:01:29; Interpreting a 3×2 matrix as a map from 2D input to 3D output; column space is a plane through the origin @ 00:01:54; Interpreting a 2×3 matrix as a map from 3D input to 2D output; landing in 2D @ 00:02:32; 1×2 matrix mapping from 2D to 1D; columns indicate landing on the number line @ 00:03:00; Explicit link between matrix encodings and dimensionality of input/output spaces @ 00:01:47",8
+BaM7OCEm3G0.youtube,Cross products as dual linear functionals: a determinant and duality journey in 3Blue1 Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra,"August 31, 2016",,"In this chapter excerpt, 3Blue1Brown unpacks the 3D cross product v × w by viewing it as the dual of a specific linear transformation from three-dimensional space to the real numbers. The talk connects determinant-based computations (via a matrix with v and w as columns and a variable first column) to a linear functional on R^3, then identifies a dual vector P whose dot product with any input vector x, y, z reproduces that determinant. The presentation alternates between a computational route—expanding a determinant and recognizing i-hat, j-hat, k-hat as the coefficients of the resulting vector—and a geometric route—seeing the dual vector as perpendicular to v and w with length equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by v and w, and using projection arguments. The discussion also situates this within the familiar 2D determinant interpretation (area) and extends to how a single-variable input yields a linear transformation; the dual vector P reconciles the two viewpoints and explains the cross product’s geometric and computational coherence. The video foreshadows a change-of-basis topic as a future direction.",Cross Product; Determinant Geometry; i-hat j-hat k-hat; Linear Duality; R3 to R Transformation; Dual Vector Functionals; 3x3 Variable Determinant; perpendicular to v & w; Parallelogram Area Length; Right-Hand Rule; Parallelogram Area; Cross Product Projection; Area Times Height; Geometric Computational Views; 2D Cross Product Area; Cross Product Volume; Input Column Linearity; Linear Functional Matrix; Change of Basis Preview,"Cross product viewed as a dual of a linear functional @ 00:00:16; Determinant expansion with i-hat, j-hat, k-hat to extract dual vector coordinates @ 00:00:29; Duality: transforming a map to R into dot product with a vector @ 01:57:00; Constructing a linear functional L(x) = det([x, v, w]) and solving for P @ 02:46:00; Geometric interpretation of dual vector: P perpendicular to v and w with magnitude equal to parallelogram area @ 10:59",9
+Ip3X9LOh2dk.youtube,Determinant as Area and Volume Scaling (2D/3D intuition),"August 10, 2016",,"This video develops an intuitive geometric meaning for the determinant. Through concrete 2D examples, it explains how a linear transformation scales area, using a diagonal matrix to show 3×2 scaling on the basis vectors and the resulting unit square mapping to a 2 by 3 rectangle (area scaling by 6). It contrasts this with a shear that preserves area, introduces the idea that the same area-scaling factor applies to any region via a grid-based approximation, and defines the determinant as that universal scaling factor. The discussion then covers negative determinants as orientation flips (with a 3D right-hand-rule perspective), the 3D analogue where the determinant scales volumes of a unit cube to a parallelepiped, and finally derives the 2×2 determinant formula ad − bc, along with a nod to the multiplicative property of determinants. The emphasis is on understanding what the determinant represents rather than memorizing a computation.",Determinant Interpretation; Area Scaling; Diagonal Basis Effect; Rectangle Area Mapping; Shear Area Preservation; Area Change Approximation; Area Scaling Factor; Zero Determinant Collapse; Negative Determinant Orientation; 3D Orientation Rule; 3D Volume Scaling; Volume Scaling Determinant; Zero Volume Dependence; 2x2 Determinant Formula; Ad and Bc Terms; Determinant Multiplicativity,Area scaling visualization with a diagonal 2×2 matrix @ 00:00:00; Area preservation under shear (parallelogram from unit square) @ 00:01:26; Grid-based universal area-change argument for determinants @ 00:01:56; Orientation flip represented by negative determinant (right-hand rule in 3D) @ 00:04:57; 3D determinant as volume scaling using unit cube to parallelepiped @ 00:05:41; 2×2 determinant formula with geometric intuition (ad − bc) @ 00:06:58; Multiplicativity of determinants (conceptual teaser for product of matrices) @ 00:09:04,8
+LyGKycYT2v0.youtube,"Dot products and duality: projection, matrices, and the dual viewpoint (Essence of Linear Algebra - Chapter 9)","August 24, 2016",,"3Blue1Brown’s Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 9, builds a deep, visualization-driven understanding of the dot product by linking its numerical definition to projection and to duality with linear transformations. The video starts with the standard coordinate-wise definition and a geometric projection interpretation, then develops intuition for why dot products are independent of the order of the operands, and how scaling affects the result. It then reframes a linear transformation from R^2 to R as a 1-by-2 matrix whose action is captured by a dot product, using i-hat, j-hat, and a diagonal embedding of the number line with a unit vector u-hat to illuminate the dual relationship between vectors and transformations. The discussion culminates in a duality-centered view: for linear transformations into the number line, there exists a unique vector whose dot product with any input vector encodes the same transformation, foreshadowing the cross product in a future example. The episode blends geometric insight with algebraic form, clarifying how dot products serve both projection intuition and a doorway to dual structures in linear algebra.",Dot Product Definition; Coordinate-wise Multiplication; Numerical Dot Product; Projection Interpretation; Projection Length; Dot Product Angle; Zero Dot Product; Dot Product Symmetry; Projection Symmetry; Dot Product Scaling; R2 to R Transformation; i Hat and j Hat; 1x2 Matrix Representation; Multiplication Analogy; 1x2 Matrix and 2D Vectors; Unit Vector Projection; Nonunit Projection; Dual Viewpoint; Vector Duality; Transformation Dual Vector; Diagonal Number Line; U-Hat Projection; Cross Product Duality,Projection length interpretation of the dot product @ 00:01:34; Order-independence intuition and symmetry for dot products @ 00:02:38; 1-by-2 matrix representation of a linear transformation in 2D @ 00:05:41; Dot product as matrix-vector multiplication (row times column) @ 00:06:31; Duality visualization: dot product encodes a transformation @ 00:11:49,7
+P2LTAUO1TdA.youtube,"Change of basis — Jennifer's coordinates (Essence of linear algebra, Chapter 13)","September 11, 2016",,"This video introduces the core idea of changing between coordinate systems by using different basis vectors. It explains how standard coordinates (i-hat, j-hat) describe vectors as linear combinations, and how an alternate basis (b1, b2) re-expresses the same vectors with different coordinates. The instructor shows how to construct a change-of-basis matrix from Jennifer’s basis, how to use its inverse to translate coordinates back, and how matrix-vector multiplication represents these coordinate shifts. A concrete example with a 90-degree rotation demonstrates how a transformation can be represented in a different basis via a conjugation A^{-1} M A, and how to compose translation, transformation, and inverse translation to obtain the same operation in Jennifer’s language. The video also emphasizes that a grid visualization is a constructed aid, not an intrinsic property of space, and it points forward to eigenvectors and eigenvalues as a next topic.",Basis Vectors; Jennifer Coordinates; Coordinate System; Coordinates as Scalars; Jennifer Basis Vectors; Change of Basis Matrix; Basis Vectors as Columns; Inverse Basis Change; Coordinate System Translation; Different Basis Coordinates; Linear Transformation; Matrix-Vector Multiplication; 90 Degree Rotation; Rotation Change of Basis; Basis Change Composition; Matrix Conjugation; Perspective Change Metaphor; Grid Visualization; Zero Vector Origin; Other Basis Coordinates; Jennifer Coordinates Example; Jennifer to Ours; Space and Coordinates; Eigenvector Preview,"Constructing the change-of-basis matrix from a nonstandard basis @ 00:05:18.040; Using the inverse change-of-basis to translate coordinates back @ 00:07:43.180; Matrix-vector multiplication as basis translation @ 00:05:25.520; Interpreting basis-change as a linear transformation between grids @ 00:06:53.380; Conjugation of a transformation for a different coordinate system (A^{-1} M A) @ 00:11:03.840; Visualizing grids as constructs, not intrinsic space @ 00:03:34.160; Relating to a known rotation matrix (90 degrees CCW) to illustrate basis-dependent coordinates @ 00:09:12.780; Empathy metaphor for basis-change (A^{-1} M A as perspective-shift) @ 00:12:16.180",4
+PFDu9oVAE-g.youtube,"Geometric and algebraic intuition for eigenvectors and eigenvalues (3Blue1Brown, Chapter 14)","September 15, 2016",,"This 3Blue1Brown episode builds intuition for eigenvectors and eigenvalues by visualizing linear transformations in the plane, then derives the algebraic condition that defines eigenpairs. It starts from a concrete 2×2 transformation, explains how some vectors stay on their own span and are simply stretched, and introduces the terms eigenvector and eigenvalue. It moves to the symbolic eigenproblem Av = λv, shows how to rewrite it as (A - λI)v = 0 and why nonzero solutions require det(A − λI) = 0, and then explores representative examples: a rotation (which may have no real eigenvectors), a shear (with a real eigenbasis along the x-axis), and a scaling matrix. Finally, it motivates the eigenbasis and diagonalization idea, showing how changing to eigenvectors as a basis diagonalizes the transformation and greatly simplifies powers like A^n, while noting that not all transformations admit a full eigenbasis. The video closes with a teaser for the next topic on abstract vector spaces.",Eigenvector Idea; Eigenvalue Trick; Linear Transformation; i Hat and j Hat; Invariant Subspaces; 2x2 Matrix Example; Characteristic Equation; Eigenvalue Equation; Eigenbasis Diagonalization; Basis Change Conjugation; Diagonal Powers; Rotation Eigenvectors; Shear Eigenstructure; Rotation Axis Eigenvector; Eigenbasis Matrix Powers; Diagonalization Limits; Eigenvector Fixed Spans; Orthogonal Diagonalization; 3Blue1Brown Style,Solve eigenpairs via the equation (A - λI)v = 0 (characteristic equation) to find λ and v @ 00:05:40.160; Determinant-based eigenvalue condition det(A − λI) = 0 to locate λ @ 00:07:24.680; Geometric interpretation: eigenvectors stay on their span and are scaled by eigenvalues @ 00:03:33.120; Diagonalization using an eigenbasis: transform A to a diagonal matrix with P⁻¹AP @ 00:15:41.640; Identify absence of real eigenvectors for a pure