+TgKwz5Ikpc8.youtube,"Abstract vector spaces: derivatives, linearity, and basis representations","September 24, 2016",,"This video meditates on what vectors are by moving beyond arrows and coordinate lists to the idea of a vector space, and then shows how linear algebra tools extend to function spaces. It treats functions as vector-like objects that can be added and scaled, defines linear transformations via additivity and scaling, and demonstrates that derivative is a linear operator. The discussion then constructs a polynomial basis for a function space, emphasizes infinite-dimensional coordinates, and shows how the derivative can be represented by a matrix in that basis. Finally, it explains vector spaces abstractly through axioms, arguing that linear algebra results apply to any vector-like system that satisfies the axioms, not just arrows in space. This bridges concrete geometric intuition with a general, coordinate-free framework increasingly used in advanced math.",Coordinates and Arrows; Basis Independence; Vector Space Axioms; Vector Space Operations; Linearity Properties; Derivative Operator; Function Vector Space; Polynomial Basis; Infinite-Dimensional Coordinates; Function Space Matrix; Derivative Matrix Construction; Inner Product Mention; Axiomatic Linear Algebra,Derivative as a linear operator @ 03:52.200; Polynomial basis for function spaces with infinite coordinates @ 07:27.760; Matrix representation of the derivative in a polynomial basis @ 09:06.700; Axioms as an abstract interface for vector spaces @ 12:20.180,9
+XkY2DOUCWMU.youtube,"Matrix multiplication as composition (Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 4)","August 8, 2016",,"This video explains how linear transformations are represented by matrices, focusing on composing transformations as a single matrix. It reviews that a linear transformation is determined by its action on the basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat) and that a matrix’s columns encode where those basis vectors land after the transformation. The core idea is that composing two linear transformations—such as a rotation followed by a shear—yields a new linear transformation that can itself be described by a matrix, the “composition matrix.” The video demonstrates constructing this matrix by tracking how i-hat and j-hat are mapped through each transformation, shows how matrix multiplication implements the sequential application of transformations (read right-to-left), and provides concrete examples (M1 and M2) to compute the composition matrix column by column. It also discusses why the order matters and how associativity of matrix multiplication arises from thinking about applying transformations, with a symbolic generalization and a teaser for extending the ideas beyond two dimensions.",Linear Transformation; Matrix Representation; Basis Vectors; I Hat Vector; j Hat; images of basis vectors; Matrix-Vector Multiplication; Transformation Composition; Rotation Composition; Shear Transformation; Composition Matrix; matrix product; Right-to-Left Evaluation; Multiplication Order; Order Matters; Associativity of Multiplication; Matrix Entries Notation; Higher Dimensional Extension,Basis vectors mapped to matrix columns (images of i-hat and j-hat) @ 00:01:33.140; Composition matrix construction from two transformations (product of matrices representing the transformations) @ 00:02:57.520; Right-to-left interpretation of matrix multiplication (function composition) @ 00:04:05.820; Column-by-column determination of the composition matrix (first column = left × first column of right; second column = left × second column of right) @ 00:06:21.960; Concrete two-matrix example (M1 and M2) to illustrate composition @ 00:05:16.420; Associativity explanation for matrix multiplication via sequential transformations @ 00:08:32.800,4
+e50Bj7jn9IQ.youtube,A Quick Trick for 2x2 Eigenvalues: Reading from Trace and Determinant,"May 7, 2021",,"This 3Blue1Brown short tutorial presents a fast method to obtain the eigenvalues of a 2x2 matrix by using two readily visible scalar properties: the trace (mean of the eigenvalues) and the determinant (product of the eigenvalues). The video derives a compact formula for the eigenvalues: if the mean of the eigenvalues is m and their product is p, then the eigenvalues are m ± sqrt(m^2 − p). Through concrete examples—including a 2x2 matrix with entries [ [3, -1], [-4, 1] ], a second matrix with mean 5 and determinant 9, and Pauli spin matrices from quantum mechanics—the method is shown to be faster than forming the characteristic polynomial in many cases. The discussion also compares this mean-product approach to the traditional method and notes where it shines (and where it may not be dramatically faster). Finally, the video hints at broader applications, such as expressing linear combinations of Pauli matrices and connecting eigenvalues to quantum observables.",Eigenvalue Trick; Eigenvector Idea; 2x2 Matrices; Matrix Trace; Matrix Determinant; Eigenvalue Sum; Eigenvalue Product; Mean of Eigenvalues; Characteristic Polynomial; Quadratic Formula; Mean Product Formula; Trace Determinant Eigenvalues; Trace Determinant Trick; Trace Determinant Formula; Pauli Spin Matrices; Quantum Mechanics; Spin Observables; Pauli Matrix Combinations; Normalized Coefficients; Matrix Exponents; Eigenvalue Method Comparison,Read eigenvalues from mean and product: the m ± sqrt(m^2 − p) formula @ 00:04:40; Apply the trick to Pauli spin matrices to reveal eigenvalues ±1 @ 00:06:53; Compare mean-product method to characteristic polynomial and identify when each is advantageous @ 00:08:41; Generalize to linear combinations of Pauli matrices with a normalized direction @ 00:09:50,4
+eu6i7WJeinw.youtube,"Cross products in 2D and 3D: area interpretation, determinants, and the right-hand rule","August 31, 2016",,"This video introduces the cross product by first revisiting its 2D interpretation as the oriented area of the parallelogram spanned by two vectors, then connects this area to determinants as a computational tool via linear transformations of the basis. It emphasizes that order matters for orientation, demonstrates how to compute 2D cross products using a determinant with V and W as columns, and explains that the true cross product in 3D is a vector whose magnitude equals the parallelogram area and whose direction is perpendicular according to the right-hand rule. A 3D determinant trick using i, j, k bases is introduced to construct the cross product vector, followed by a brief discussion of the rationale behind this notation and a teaser for a deeper geometric interpretation via duality in a subsequent video.",Cross Product Area; Cross Product Orientation; Cross Product Order; i-hat cross j-hat orientation; 2D Cross Product Determinant; Area Scaling Determinant; V and W Columns; Parallelogram Mapping; 2D Cross Product Example; Cross Product Magnitude; Cross Direction Rule; Cross Product Scaling; Vector Cross Product; 3D Determinant Method; Duality Teaser,"2D cross product via area of parallelogram and determinant @ 00:02:37; Right-hand rule to determine direction of cross product @ 00:05:50; 3D determinant trick using i, j, k in the first column @ 00:06:45; Determinant as linear transformation area mapping (unit square to parallelogram) @ 00:03:11",10
+fNk_zzaMoSs.youtube,"Vectors: Coordinate framing, addition, and scaling (3Blue1Brown – Essence of Linear Algebra, Chapter 1)","August 5, 2016",,"3Blue1Brown’s Vector chapter introduces vectors from multiple angles (physics as arrows, computer science as lists, and the mathematician’s abstraction), then grounds them in a concrete 2D/3D coordinate frame rooted at the origin. The video shows how vector coordinates encode steps from origin to a tip, distinguishes vectors from points, and explains two core operations—vector addition via a tip-to-tail construction and scalar multiplication (scaling) that stretches, shrinks, or reverses direction. It emphasizes translating between geometric arrows and their coordinate lists, illustrates these ideas with explicit numerical examples, and discusses how these foundations enable data visualization and space manipulation in physics and graphics, while previewing future topics like span, bases, and linear dependence.",Vector Perspectives; Vectors in Space; Free vs Rooted Vectors; Cartesian Coordinate System; Origin and Axes; Origin-to-Point Coordinates; Vectors and Points; 2D and 3D Vectors; Ordered Pairs and Triplets; 3D Axis Directions; Grid Coordinate Frames; Vectors as Number Lists; Tip-to-Tail Addition; Vector Sum Interpretation; Vector Addition Motion; Coordinate Addition; Vector Addition Example; Vector Scaling; Scalar Multiplication; Scaling Length Direction; Scalars and Vectors; Core Linear Operations; Geometry Coordinate Translation; Why Linear Algebra Matters; Span Basis Dependence,Tip-to-tail vector addition (geometric construction) @ 00:04:53; Coordinate-wise depiction of vector sums (component-wise interpretation) @ 00:06:39; Scalar multiplication as scaling (length and direction changes) @ 00:07:01; Dual interpretation: vectors as arrows and as coordinate lists @ 00:02:40;  Bridging geometry and numbers to unify data visualization and space manipulation @ 00:09:11,5
+jBsC34PxzoM.youtube,Geometric derivation of Cramer's rule using determinants and parallelograms,"March 16, 2019",,"This video provides a geometric interpretation of solving linear systems via determinants, focusing on a 2x2 system and introducing what is known in the curriculum as Cramer's rule (referred to as Kramer's rule in the transcript). It frames the system as a linear transformation whose output is a linear combination of the matrix columns, discusses the significance of a nonzero determinant for invertibility, and introduces a visually intuitive method: treating coordinates as areas and volumes (parallelograms and parallelepipeds) that scale by the determinant under the transformation. The speaker shows how to recover input coordinates from the output by forming determinants of modified matrices, demonstrates with a quick numerical check (x=3, y=2), and notes how this approach generalizes to higher dimensions while acknowledging Gaussian elimination as the more practical computation method. The emphasis is on deepening conceptual understanding of determinants and their geometric meaning rather than prescribing a routine algorithm for computation.",Linear Systems; 2x2 Linear System; Matrix as Transformation; Cramers Rule Derivation; Nonzero Cramers Rule Derivation; Singular Determinant Case; Orthonormal Dot Product; Dot Product Preservation; Cramer Area Coordinate; Signed Area Coordinates; Parallelepiped Volume; Area Volume Scaling; Modified Determinants; Cramer's Rule; Gaussian Elimination Alternative; Higher Dimensional Matrices,Cramer's rule (determinant-based solution of linear systems) @ 00:10:18; Determinant as area/volume scaling under a linear transformation @ 00:08:07; Solving coordinates via determinants of modified matrices @ 00:09:04,7
+k7RM-ot2NWY.youtube,"Linear combinations, span, and basis vectors (Essence of linear algebra, Chapter 2)","August 6, 2016",,"This video introduces a central viewpoint in linear algebra: coordinates are best understood as scalars that scale basis vectors, so any vector is a linear combination of basis vectors. It names the x- and y-axes basis vectors i-hat and j-hat, and shows how changing the chosen basis changes the coordinate representation. The core ideas unfold through the concepts of linear combinations, spans, and the distinction between dependent and independent sets of vectors. The discussion extends from two-dimensional space to three dimensions, illustrating how new vectors can expand the span from a plane to the entire space, and it culminates in the precise definition of a basis as a linearly independent spanning set, setting up the upcoming topic of matrices and space transformations.",i Hat and j Hat; Interpreting as Scalars; Linear Combinations; Plane Span of Two Vectors; Vector Dependence; Vector Combinations; Coordinate Systems; Span as Plane; Arrows and Points; Span Extension; Adding a Third Vector; 3D Span Conditions; Basis Definition; Linear Transformations Preview,"Expressing coordinates as scalars multiplying unit basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat) @ 00:01:01; Visualizing span as a geometric sheet (plane) or line in space @ 00:04:14; Comparing cases: span of two vectors in 2D vs. extension with a third vector to reach 3D @ 00:06:25; Distinguishing linear dependence and independence to assess redundancy @ 00:08:16; Defining a basis as a linearly independent spanning set and tying to matrices @ 00:09:06",7
+kYB8IZa5AuE.youtube,"Understanding 2D Linear Transformations: Basis Vectors, 2x2 Matrices, and Space-Mapping","August 7, 2016",,"This video introduces the core idea that a two-dimensional linear transformation is a function that maps vectors in the plane to other vectors in the plane, visualized by moving points (basis-free) or, more intuitively, by tracking where the basis vectors i-hat and j-hat land. It emphasizes visual understanding via an infinite grid and explains why linear transformations preserve straight lines and keep the origin fixed. The key insight is that the images of i-hat and j-hat completely determine the transformation, enabling calculation of any vector’s image through a simple linear combination. This leads to the 2x2 matrix representation, where the two columns encode the transformed basis vectors, and matrix-vector multiplication becomes the rule for applying the transformation. Concrete examples include a 90-degree rotation and a shear. The video also covers how to deduce the transformation from a given matrix and what happens when the images of the basis vectors are linearly dependent. The takeaway is that matrices describe space-transforming operations with a small, interpretable set of numbers, setting the stage for later topics like matrix multiplication, determinants, and eigenvalues.",Transformation vs Function; 2D Transformation Visualization; Vectors as Points; Infinite Grid Visualization; Grid Preservation; Origin Fixed Transform; Linear vs Nonlinear; i Hat and j Hat; Transformation from Basis Images; Vector Mapping Combinations; 2D Transformation Matrix; 2x2 Matrix Representation; Basis Images as Columns; Applying Transformations; Matrix Coefficient Notation; 90 Degree Rotation Matrix; Shear Matrix; Matrix Transformation; Dependent Basis Images; Space Transformations; Determinants and Eigenvalues; Matrix Multiplication Preview,"Visualize linear transformations by tracking images of basis vectors (i-hat, j-hat) @ 00:04:12; Read a 2x2 matrix as the transformed basis vectors (columns are i-hat', j-hat') @ 00:06:23; Compute the image of any vector via linear combination using basis images @ 00:04:12; Matrix-vector multiplication as the operational rule for applying the transformation @ 00:06:41; Concrete examples: rotation by 90 degrees and a shear as explicit matrices @ 00:08:13; Deduce transformation from a given matrix by examining i-hat and j-hat mappings @ 00:09:08",10
+rHLEWRxRGiM.youtube,"Three-dimensional linear transformations: basis vectors, 3x3 matrices, and 3D rotations","August 9, 2016",,"This 3Blue1Brown tutorial extends the familiar two-dimensional view of linear transformations to three dimensions. It emphasizes that, as with 2D, a linear transformation in 3D is fully described by how it sends the three standard basis vectors i-hat, j-hat, and k-hat, with those images becoming the columns of a 3x3 matrix. The video shows how to map any 3D vector by combining scaled basis vectors and summing the results, and it explains that applying two transformations corresponds to multiplying their 3x3 matrices (right-to-left). A concrete example rotates space by 90 degrees about the y-axis, illustrating how the basis vectors transform and how that information forms the matrix. The discussion also motivates the relevance of 3D matrix multiplication in fields like computer graphics and robotics, and it foreshadows the determinant as a future topic.",3D Linear Transformations; 3x3 Matrices; i j k Basis; 3D Vector Transformation; Y-Axis Rotation; Columns as Basis Images; Transformation Composition; Matrix Multiplication Order; 3D Grid Visualization; Origin Preservation; Computer Graphics; Robotics Applications; 2D to 3D Intuition; 3D Coordinate System,"90-degree rotation around the y-axis demonstrated as a basis-vector mapping (i-hat -> z-axis, j-hat unchanged, k-hat -> x-axis); matrix columns become the transformation @ 00:02:24; constructing a 3x3 matrix by placing the images of i-hat, j-hat, k-hat as its columns @ 00:02:11; applying a 3x3 matrix to a vector via its coordinates (scaling basis vectors and summing) @ 00:02:56; interpreting matrix multiplication as applying one transformation after another (composition of transformations) @ 00:03:39",7
+uQhTuRlWMxw.youtube,"Inverse matrices, column space and null space explained through linear transformations","August 15, 2016",,"This video presents a conceptual treatment of inverse matrices, column space, and null space by reframing linear systems as linear transformations. It emphasizes intuition over computation, noting that Gaussian elimination and related methods exist elsewhere, and that software is often used for actual calculations. The discussion introduces Ax = v as a single geometric problem: finding the input x that lands on v after applying the transformation A. It distinguishes the nonzero-determinant case, where an inverse exists and a unique solution can be obtained by applying A^{-1} to v, from the zero-determinant case, where the transformation squashes space to a lower dimension and an inverse does not exist. It then defines the column space as the span of A’s columns (the possible outputs), introduces rank as the dimensionality of that output, and defines the null space (kernel) as the set of vectors that map to the zero vector. The video emphasizes four main ideas: the geometric view of linear systems via transformations, when inverses exist and how to solve using inverse transformations, what column space and rank tell us about reachability, and what null space tells us about the set of all solutions. Acknowledgment is made that the scope is limited to square systems (same number of equations and unknowns) and that non-square cases will appear later.",Linear Systems; Ax Equals v Form; Matrix A Transformation; Vector x and v; Inverse Matrix; Determinant Invertibility; Identity Transformation; Inverse Solution; Matrix Rank; Column Space; Column Space Span; Full Rank; Null Space; Nonsquare Output Dimensions; 2x2 and 3x3 Examples; Zero Determinant Cases; Geometric Intuition; Non-square Matrices Later; Linear Systems Geometry; Computation Tools,Inverse transformation as the reverse operation @ 00:04:34; Solving Ax = v by applying A^{-1} to v @ 00:05:29; Rank as the dimension of the column space @ 00:08:14; Null space as the set of vectors mapping to zero @ 00:10:32,4
+v8VSDg_WQlA.youtube,"Nonsquare matrices as transformations between dimensions | Chapter 8, Essence of linear algebra","August 16, 2016",,"This footnote to the Essence of Linear Algebra series explains how non-square matrices encode linear transformations between spaces of different dimensions. It shows that transforming 2D inputs to 3D outputs is represented by a 3×2 matrix, whose columns are the images of the input basis vectors, with explicit examples (I-hat maps to (2, -1, -2) and J-hat maps to (0, 1, 1)). The video then reviews how the shape of the matrix reflects input vs. output dimensionality (3×2 for 2D→3D, 2×3 for 3D→2D) and discusses a 2D→1D case encoded by a 1×2 matrix. Throughout, the geometric idea is that linearity preserves parallel grid lines and the origin, while inputs and outputs live in distinct vector spaces. The talk also notes that this type of transformation connects meaningfully to the dot product, which will be explored in the next video, and invites viewers to experiment with interpreting matrix multiplication and linear systems in cross-dimensional contexts.",Non-square Matrices; Dimension Transformations; Input Output Dimensions; Basis Vectors; Matrix Encoding; Basis Vector Images; 3x2 Matrix Transformations; 2x3 Matrix Transformations; 1x2 Matrix Transformations; Column Space Interpretation; Full Rank; Origin Mapping; Grid Linearity; Dot Product Teaser,Matrix encoding of a linear transformation via images of basis vectors @ 00:01:27; Interpreting matrix shape as mapping between input and output dimensions @ 00:01:53; Interpreting 3×2 and 2×3 matrices for cross-dimensional mappings @ 00:02:41; 1×2 matrix as a 2D→1D transformation (basis landing coordinates) @ 00:03:50,5
